00:00:00.510 --> 00:00:01.590
MIKE COLEMAN:
Organizations today

00:00:01.590 --> 00:00:03.180
are looking for
ways to streamline

00:00:03.180 --> 00:00:06.097
the migration of existing
applications to Kubernetes.

00:00:06.097 --> 00:00:08.430
In this video, we're going
to look at a couple offerings

00:00:08.430 --> 00:00:10.260
from Google Cloud
that can drastically

00:00:10.260 --> 00:00:13.050
reduce the complexity around
getting existing workloads

00:00:13.050 --> 00:00:14.640
running in Kubernetes--

00:00:14.640 --> 00:00:17.610
Migrate for GKE
and GKE Autopilot.

00:00:29.150 --> 00:00:32.299
Let's start with a brief
overview of GKE Autopilot.

00:00:32.299 --> 00:00:34.280
Kubernetes is
extremely powerful,

00:00:34.280 --> 00:00:36.830
but can be a challenge to
setup and manage by yourself.

00:00:36.830 --> 00:00:39.680
Provisioning and managing
resources, security and network

00:00:39.680 --> 00:00:42.680
configuration, and scaling
can all be challenging.

00:00:42.680 --> 00:00:44.480
To help alleviate
some of these issues,

00:00:44.480 --> 00:00:47.630
Google Cloud has long offered
Google Kubernetes Engine

00:00:47.630 --> 00:00:49.070
as a managed service.

00:00:49.070 --> 00:00:52.310
By using GKE in standard
mode, customers still

00:00:52.310 --> 00:00:54.500
provision and manage the
underlying infrastructure,

00:00:54.500 --> 00:00:56.720
but the vast majority
of the configuration

00:00:56.720 --> 00:00:57.980
is handled by Google.

00:00:57.980 --> 00:01:00.050
This is a great
solution for customers

00:01:00.050 --> 00:01:02.230
who still want to maintain
a degree of control--

00:01:02.230 --> 00:01:05.150
for example, if they want
to use GPU instances.

00:01:05.150 --> 00:01:07.910
However, for customers who want
to run mainstream workloads

00:01:07.910 --> 00:01:10.370
and don't want to worry about
configuring infrastructure,

00:01:10.370 --> 00:01:13.970
Google Cloud recently
introduced GKE Autopilot.

00:01:13.970 --> 00:01:16.268
With Autopilot mode
clusters, customers

00:01:16.268 --> 00:01:18.560
don't have to worry about
the underlying infrastructure

00:01:18.560 --> 00:01:19.670
at all.

00:01:19.670 --> 00:01:23.150
Google Cloud essentially becomes
your site reliability engineer,

00:01:23.150 --> 00:01:25.430
scaling the cluster
up and down as needed.

00:01:25.430 --> 00:01:28.100
You simply choose which
workloads need to run there,

00:01:28.100 --> 00:01:31.235
and Google Cloud does a bunch
of the heavy lifting for you.

00:01:31.235 --> 00:01:34.820
GKE Autopilot provides several
key benefits to customers.

00:01:34.820 --> 00:01:37.580
First, optimize production
like a Kubernetes expert.

00:01:37.580 --> 00:01:39.230
Google creates
clusters according

00:01:39.230 --> 00:01:41.390
to battle-tested and
hardened best practices that

00:01:41.390 --> 00:01:43.460
yield optimized
configuration and security

00:01:43.460 --> 00:01:45.650
posture, ready for
production, helping

00:01:45.650 --> 00:01:49.310
you reduce GKE learning curve
and get to production faster.

00:01:49.310 --> 00:01:51.050
Strong security posture.

00:01:51.050 --> 00:01:54.080
Google takes full responsibility
for nodes and infrastructure

00:01:54.080 --> 00:01:56.420
security, eliminating
infrastructure security

00:01:56.420 --> 00:01:57.860
management tasks.

00:01:57.860 --> 00:01:59.810
Reduce day 2 operations.

00:01:59.810 --> 00:02:02.750
Google monitors the entire
Autopilot cluster-- control

00:02:02.750 --> 00:02:05.690
plane, worker nodes, and core
Kubernetes system components--

00:02:05.690 --> 00:02:08.690
ensuring your pods
are always scheduled.

00:02:08.690 --> 00:02:11.000
Improve resource efficiency.

00:02:11.000 --> 00:02:13.820
Google takes full responsibility
for optimizing your resource

00:02:13.820 --> 00:02:14.510
consumption.

00:02:14.510 --> 00:02:17.630
You pay only for the
pods, not for the nodes.

00:02:17.630 --> 00:02:21.170
So you have an easy way to run
Kubernetes with GKE Autopilot.

00:02:21.170 --> 00:02:24.890
How can you easily get existing
workloads onto those clusters?

00:02:24.890 --> 00:02:27.350
For those of you not familiar
with Migrate for GKE,

00:02:27.350 --> 00:02:29.330
this solution can be
a really useful tool

00:02:29.330 --> 00:02:32.570
in your app modernization tool
set to accelerate and simplify

00:02:32.570 --> 00:02:36.350
your enterprise's application
modernization journey to GKE.

00:02:36.350 --> 00:02:38.450
You have the option
to use Migrate for GKE

00:02:38.450 --> 00:02:41.150
as is to accelerate your
end-customer modernization

00:02:41.150 --> 00:02:43.250
or build your proprietary
automations on top

00:02:43.250 --> 00:02:45.230
of the product to
increase productivity

00:02:45.230 --> 00:02:47.285
of your modernization projects.

00:02:47.285 --> 00:02:49.082
Either way, it can
drastically reduce

00:02:49.082 --> 00:02:50.540
the time it takes
to move workloads

00:02:50.540 --> 00:02:53.090
from VMs into containers.

00:02:53.090 --> 00:02:55.520
When looking at migrating
an existing workload,

00:02:55.520 --> 00:02:58.100
there are four discrete steps
that you'll want to go through.

00:02:58.100 --> 00:03:00.140
First is discovery
and assessment.

00:03:00.140 --> 00:03:02.030
Migrate has a fit
assessment tool

00:03:02.030 --> 00:03:03.560
that can be used
to help identify

00:03:03.560 --> 00:03:06.050
what workloads you have
running and which are the best

00:03:06.050 --> 00:03:07.490
candidate for migration.

00:03:07.490 --> 00:03:08.990
Look into the fit
assessment tool

00:03:08.990 --> 00:03:10.980
video for more information.

00:03:10.980 --> 00:03:12.620
Next is setup and planning.

00:03:12.620 --> 00:03:14.750
Migrated workloads
can run on GKE

00:03:14.750 --> 00:03:17.090
in standard or Autopilot
mode, as well as

00:03:17.090 --> 00:03:18.410
on Anthos and even Cloud Run.

00:03:18.410 --> 00:03:21.830
Additionally, you can
orchestrate migration pipelines

00:03:21.830 --> 00:03:24.390
to help automate the
migration process.

00:03:24.390 --> 00:03:26.940
In this video, we're
focused on the third phase,

00:03:26.940 --> 00:03:28.880
so I won't spend a
lot of time here.

00:03:28.880 --> 00:03:31.940
The final phase deals with
how you manage applications

00:03:31.940 --> 00:03:33.440
after they have been migrated.

00:03:33.440 --> 00:03:36.140
Because Migrate for GKE
provides you with the resulting

00:03:36.140 --> 00:03:38.720
Dockerfile, you can
easily manage updates

00:03:38.720 --> 00:03:42.050
to your code via your
standard deployment pipelines.

00:03:42.050 --> 00:03:43.610
Drilling down a
bit into the steps

00:03:43.610 --> 00:03:45.110
associated with
that third phase,

00:03:45.110 --> 00:03:46.820
and what you'll see
in the upcoming demo,

00:03:46.820 --> 00:03:49.520
is the general Migrate
for GKE workflow

00:03:49.520 --> 00:03:51.890
when it comes to an
actual migration.

00:03:51.890 --> 00:03:55.070
First, we're going to create
a GKE Autopilot cluster.

00:03:55.070 --> 00:03:58.790
Then we're going to provide
Migrate for GKE a source VM.

00:03:58.790 --> 00:04:01.160
The migration processes
will inspect the VM

00:04:01.160 --> 00:04:02.810
and then generate
a migration plan

00:04:02.810 --> 00:04:04.370
that we can edit as needed.

00:04:04.370 --> 00:04:06.780
From there, it will generate
the deployment artifacts,

00:04:06.780 --> 00:04:08.420
including a Docker container.

00:04:08.420 --> 00:04:12.020
We'll then deploy that container
onto our Autopilot cluster.

00:04:12.020 --> 00:04:14.190
With that, let's
jump into our demo.

00:04:14.190 --> 00:04:17.160
We'll start the demo by building
out our Autopilot cluster.

00:04:17.160 --> 00:04:18.818
Notice that all I
need to do is provide

00:04:18.818 --> 00:04:20.360
a name for the
cluster and the region

00:04:20.360 --> 00:04:21.485
in which it's going to run.

00:04:24.010 --> 00:04:25.760
And now that our
cluster's up and running,

00:04:25.760 --> 00:04:28.690
we can move on to deploying
our sample application.

00:04:28.690 --> 00:04:30.782
OK, so let's deploy
the Kubernetes YAML.

00:04:30.782 --> 00:04:32.740
And notice that's creating
a bunch of services.

00:04:32.740 --> 00:04:34.570
And while they're
created, Autopilot

00:04:34.570 --> 00:04:37.450
is increasing the resource
requirements automatically

00:04:37.450 --> 00:04:39.150
for us to address
those new services.

00:04:39.150 --> 00:04:40.900
Now that that's run,
let's check our pods.

00:04:40.900 --> 00:04:41.755
They're still in Pending.

00:04:41.755 --> 00:04:43.540
Let's check them again
here a little bit later,

00:04:43.540 --> 00:04:44.915
and everything is
up and running.

00:04:44.915 --> 00:04:46.538
So we can test the service now.

00:04:46.538 --> 00:04:48.580
I'm going to get the
frontend service IP address.

00:04:48.580 --> 00:04:50.410
I'm going to navigate
into my web browser.

00:04:50.410 --> 00:04:52.600
And you can see here the
application is running.

00:04:52.600 --> 00:04:53.350
Let me go in.

00:04:53.350 --> 00:04:56.470
I'm going to buy an item, and
then I'm going to check out.

00:04:56.470 --> 00:04:58.390
And you see everything
working exactly

00:04:58.390 --> 00:04:59.590
as you would expect it to.

00:04:59.590 --> 00:05:02.230
The way the application
works is every service

00:05:02.230 --> 00:05:05.080
except for the payment service
is running in Kubernetes.

00:05:05.080 --> 00:05:07.030
The payment service
is in a VM, and that's

00:05:07.030 --> 00:05:08.197
what we're going to migrate.

00:05:08.197 --> 00:05:10.850
But first, we need to stop
that VM so we can migrate it.

00:05:10.850 --> 00:05:12.490
So let's do that now.

00:05:12.490 --> 00:05:14.950
To perform the actual migration,
I switched over to Migrate

00:05:14.950 --> 00:05:15.730
for Containers.

00:05:15.730 --> 00:05:17.440
And I'm going to click
Create Migration.

00:05:17.440 --> 00:05:19.130
I'll give my migration a name.

00:05:19.130 --> 00:05:21.130
I'll select a source
that I created earlier.

00:05:21.130 --> 00:05:22.390
It's a Linux VM.

00:05:22.390 --> 00:05:23.860
We'll give it that VM name.

00:05:23.860 --> 00:05:25.450
And we're going to
migrate the image.

00:05:25.450 --> 00:05:27.550
With that, I'll click
Create Migration.

00:05:27.550 --> 00:05:28.630
And this will go off.

00:05:28.630 --> 00:05:31.130
It'll take a little bit of time,
so I'll speed up the video,

00:05:31.130 --> 00:05:33.460
and I'll rejoin you
when it's finished.

00:05:33.460 --> 00:05:35.890
Our migration is completed,
so what I'm going to do now

00:05:35.890 --> 00:05:37.432
is I'm going to come
in and I'm going

00:05:37.432 --> 00:05:40.150
to review and edit the migration
plan that was generated.

00:05:40.150 --> 00:05:41.260
What we're going to
do first is we're

00:05:41.260 --> 00:05:42.677
going to come in
and make a change

00:05:42.677 --> 00:05:46.030
to this v2kServiceManager
variable and set that to true.

00:05:46.030 --> 00:05:49.810
That allows us to use
the enhanced runtime

00:05:49.810 --> 00:05:51.902
to run on GKE Autopilot.

00:05:51.902 --> 00:05:53.860
And we're going to change
the name of the image

00:05:53.860 --> 00:05:55.930
from Payment Service
VM to Payment Service,

00:05:55.930 --> 00:05:58.060
and the same thing for
the Kubernetes service,

00:05:58.060 --> 00:06:00.010
since it's no longer
running in a VM.

00:06:00.010 --> 00:06:01.720
We'll generate our artifacts.

00:06:01.720 --> 00:06:04.000
That takes a while, so let
me speed up the video here,

00:06:04.000 --> 00:06:06.840
and I'll rejoin you
when it's complete.

00:06:06.840 --> 00:06:08.553
So this process is
almost wrapped up.

00:06:08.553 --> 00:06:10.970
What we're going to do here
is we're going to take a look,

00:06:10.970 --> 00:06:13.490
after it finishes, at the
artifacts that were generated.

00:06:13.490 --> 00:06:15.800
You can see we have a
couple of Docker images,

00:06:15.800 --> 00:06:18.960
as well as the Dockerfile
and some other artifacts.

00:06:18.960 --> 00:06:21.200
I'm going to download
those into Cloud Shell.

00:06:21.200 --> 00:06:23.390
So I'll get-artifacts
from my-migration.

00:06:23.390 --> 00:06:26.303
If I look in the directory,
you can see those artifacts

00:06:26.303 --> 00:06:27.470
that I mentioned previously.

00:06:27.470 --> 00:06:31.440
Let's apply the deployment
spec that was generated.

00:06:31.440 --> 00:06:33.740
This is going to deploy
our new payment service,

00:06:33.740 --> 00:06:37.130
so we'll do a kubectl
apply -f deployment_spec.

00:06:37.130 --> 00:06:40.310
That's creating the new
service and the new deployment

00:06:40.310 --> 00:06:41.185
in Kubernetes.

00:06:41.185 --> 00:06:42.560
Clear the screen,
and let's check

00:06:42.560 --> 00:06:44.600
and see the status
of those pods.

00:06:44.600 --> 00:06:46.220
This might take a few seconds.

00:06:48.800 --> 00:06:50.800
All right, let's just
wait for those to come up,

00:06:50.800 --> 00:06:52.008
and we'll speed up the video.

00:06:55.480 --> 00:06:57.760
Now that that is up and
running, that new service,

00:06:57.760 --> 00:06:58.720
what we're going to
do is we're going

00:06:58.720 --> 00:07:01.220
to check our app to see if it
actually is all working again.

00:07:01.220 --> 00:07:02.470
So we'll buy this camera.

00:07:02.470 --> 00:07:04.360
Add to cart.

00:07:04.360 --> 00:07:05.980
Place order.

00:07:05.980 --> 00:07:08.230
And everything is working
exactly as expected.

00:07:08.230 --> 00:07:10.240
We have migrated the
payment service from the VM

00:07:10.240 --> 00:07:11.860
into the container.

00:07:11.860 --> 00:07:13.600
I hope that demo
showed you how easy it

00:07:13.600 --> 00:07:16.660
can be to not only deploy
a GKE Autopilot cluster,

00:07:16.660 --> 00:07:18.580
but also how simple
it can be to migrate

00:07:18.580 --> 00:07:21.370
an existing VM-based
workload to Kubernetes.

00:07:21.370 --> 00:07:23.320
For more detailed
information, check out

00:07:23.320 --> 00:07:26.050
our documentation, as well as
the other videos in our Migrate

00:07:26.050 --> 00:07:26.680
series.

00:07:26.680 --> 00:07:28.300
And don't forget to
like and subscribe

00:07:28.300 --> 00:07:31.180
to be alerted whenever
we publish new content.

